Programs spend most of their time in inner loops, so these five transformations give the biggest speed-ups.

(i) Loop-invariant code motion
A computation whose value does not change inside the loop is moved to just before the loop (the preheader).


 for (i = 0; i < n; i++) t = x * y; // computed once
 a[i] = x * y + i; for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
 a[i] = t + i;



(ii) Loop unrolling
Repeat the body to reduce loop-control overhead (tests, increments, jumps).


 i = 1; i = 1;
 while (i <= 100) { a[i] = b[i]; i++; } while (i <= 100) {
 a[i] = b[i]; i++;
 a[i] = b[i]; i++;
 } // 50 tests instead of
 100



(iii) Induction-variable elimination
Variables that change by a fixed amount each iteration (i, and j = 4*i) are induction variables. Keep only one and rewrite the test in
terms of it.


 i = 1; t1 = 4;
 L: t1 = 4 * i; L: x = a[t1];
 x = a[t1]; t1 = t1 + 4;
 i = i + 1; if (t1 <= 80) goto L; // i eliminated
 if (i <= 20) goto L;



(iv) Loop jamming (loop fusion)
Merge two adjacent loops with the same range into one, halving loop overhead.


 for (i = 0; i < n; i++) a[i] = 0; for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
 for (i = 0; i < n; i++) b[i] = 1; a[i] = 0;
 b[i] = 1;
 }


Allowed only if the second loop does not need values the first loop computes in a later iteration.

(v) Strength reduction
Replace an expensive operation by a cheaper one, typically multiplication by addition inside loops.




